---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="ExCSV — CSV, but self-describing"
  description="Types, units, stats, and SQL travel with the rows. The data is still CSV."
  wide
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-text">
      <h1>CSV, but self-describing</h1>
      <p class="tagline">
        Types, units, stats, and SQL sit in the file — not in a README.
        The rows stay CSV. pandas, Excel, and <code>awk</code> don't notice.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href="/examples/" class="btn btn-primary">See examples</a>
        <a href="/variants/" class="btn btn-secondary">Formats</a>
      </div>
      <p class="version-tag">v0.4 — Draft</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-code">
      <pre><code><span class="hl-meta">#!excsv version=0.4 delim=comma header=1 rows=4 sql-dialect=postgres</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#@source: analytics_db.events</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=date type=date</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=event type=string</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=count type=int</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#$ddl: CREATE TABLE events (date DATE, event TEXT, count INTEGER)</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#%sum: ,,1250</span>
date,event,count
2026-01-15,signup,320
2026-01-15,purchase,180
2026-01-16,signup,410
2026-01-16,purchase,340</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>What's in the file</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      Tabular exports are everywhere; the note explaining them isn't.
      You infer types, rescan for totals, rebuild DDL from memory.
      ExCSV is that note — written as <code>#</code> lines any CSV reader already skips.
    </p>
    <div class="cards cards-3">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Still CSV</h3>
        <p>Metadata is comments. The data section is ordinary rows. Nothing breaks.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Types &amp; units</h3>
        <p><code>01720</code> is a ZIP code. <code>1050.50</code> is USD. Write it once, stop guessing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Stats in the file</h3>
        <p>Sum, avg, count, min/max on <code>#%</code> lines. The total is a fact, not a second pass over 2M rows.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>SQL travels with it</h3>
        <p>DDL and queries, tagged <code>postgres</code> / <code>mysql</code> / <code>clickhouse</code>. Recreate the table where you're loading.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Unix tools just work</h3>
        <p><code>grep</code>, <code>awk</code>, <code>cut</code> on the rows. pandas: <code>comment="#"</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Add layers as you go</h3>
        <p>Header, then types, then stats, then SQL. Stop whenever it's useful. Line-oriented <code>key=value</code> — not a nested blob.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>Five ways to ship it</h2>
    <p class="lead">Same language. Different packaging — whether you can touch the CSV, how big it is, one table or several — plus a JSON serialization for when the consumer doesn't speak CSV.</p>
    <div class="cards cards-5">
      <a class="card card-link" href="/variants/#plain">
        <span class="ext">.excsv</span>
        <h3>Plain</h3>
        <p>One file. Schema and rows together. The default.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card card-link" href="/variants/#sidecar">
        <span class="ext">.excsv + .csv</span>
        <h3>Sidecar</h3>
        <p>Annotate a CSV you can't — or won't — modify.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card card-link" href="/variants/#zip">
        <span class="ext">.excsv.zip</span>
        <h3>ZIP</h3>
        <p>Compress a table. Peek schema and sums without unzipping.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card card-link" href="/variants/#pack">
        <span class="ext">.excsv.pack.zip</span>
        <h3>Pack</h3>
        <p>Wide tables and multi-table snapshots, column by column.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card card-link" href="/variants/#json">
        <span class="ext">.excsv.json</span>
        <h3>JSON</h3>
        <p>The same document as JSON, for APIs and AI output.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <p class="kicker">LLM-friendly</p>
    <h2>Made for AI assistants, too</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      Paste a table into a chat. The model gets schema, types, units, and totals up front —
      no guessing the delimiter, no inferring types from five rows, no recomputing the sum.
    </p>
    <div class="cards cards-2">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Types &amp; units</h3>
        <p>An LLM reads <code>1050.50</code> as USD, not a count, and doesn't treat a ZIP code as an integer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Aggregations as a check</h3>
        <p><code>#%</code> rows carry sum, avg, count. The model answers — and sanity-checks its own math — without scanning every row.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Grain, spelled out</h3>
        <p><code>#@grain: one row per order</code> says what a row <em>is</em>, instead of forcing the model to reverse-engineer it from column names.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Roles &amp; enums</h3>
        <p><code>role=</code> (id / dimension / measure / time) and <code>enum=</code> tell it what to group by, what to sum, and what not to invent.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <pre class="snippet"><code><span class="hl-meta">#@grain: one row per order</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=order_id type=int role=id</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=status type=string role=dimension enum=pending|completed|cancelled</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=amount type=decimal unit=USD role=measure agg=sum</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#%sum: ,,1050.50</span></code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="block inspired">
    <h2>Inspired by</h2>
    <div class="cards cards-3">
      <div>
        <h3><a href="https://docs.astropy.org/en/stable/io/ascii/ecsv.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ECSV</a> — Astropy</h3>
        <p>A CSV can carry a versioned header and per-column schema as <code>#</code> lines and still round-trip through every plain reader. ExCSV keeps the shape, swaps nested YAML for <code>key=value</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3><a href="https://docs.influxdata.com/influxdb/v2/reference/syntax/annotated-csv/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Annotated CSV</a> — InfluxDB</h3>
        <p>One annotation row, one value per data column. ExCSV's <code>#%</code> aggregations (<code>sum</code>, <code>avg</code>, <code>min</code>, …) inherit that layout.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3><a href="https://github.com/MetaCSV/MetaCSV" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MetaCSV</a> — sidecar</h3>
        <p>Typed metadata beside a plain <code>data.csv</code>. Same idea; ExCSV uses <code>#</code> lines and <code>reference=</code> instead of a second CSV vocabulary. See <a href="/variants/#sidecar">sidecar</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="credit">Full credit and a field mapping on the <a href="/spec/#prior-art">guide</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 2.5rem 0 3.25rem;
  }
  .hero h1 {
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    margin-top: 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }
  .tagline {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 28rem;
  }
  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem 0.65rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .version-tag {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 0.55em 1.1em;
    border-radius: 8px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: opacity 0.15s, border-color 0.15s, filter 0.15s;
  }
  .btn:hover { text-decoration: none; }
  .btn-primary {
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: #fff;
  }
  .btn-primary:hover { filter: brightness(1.05); }
  :global(html[data-theme="dark"]) .btn-primary {
    color: #111;
  }
  .btn-secondary {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    color: var(--color-text);
    background: var(--color-bg);
  }
  .btn-secondary:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-muted);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }
  .hero-code pre {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .block {
    padding-bottom: 2.75rem;
  }
  .block h2 {
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .kicker {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }
  .lead {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .cards {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .cards-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  .cards-3 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  .cards-4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); }
  .cards-5 { grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr); }

  .card {
    background: var(--color-card-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.25rem 1.3rem;
  }
  .card h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
  }
  .card p {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .card-link {
    display: block;
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .card-link:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .ext {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin-bottom: 0.45rem;
  }

  .snippet {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
  }

  .inspired {
    padding-top: 1.75rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .inspired .cards {
    gap: 1.5rem 1.75rem;
  }
  .inspired .cards > div {
    background: none;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .inspired h3 {
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
  }
  .inspired h3 a {
    color: var(--color-text);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .inspired h3 a:hover { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .inspired p {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .credit {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 1100px) {
    .cards-5 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 960px) {
    .hero {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2rem;
      padding: 1.5rem 0 2.5rem;
    }
    .cards-4,
    .cards-5 { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .hero h1 { font-size: 1.8rem; }
    .cards-3,
    .cards-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .cards-4,
    .cards-5 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .cta-row {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
    }
    .btn {
      width: 100%;
      justify-content: center;
    }
  }
</style>
